<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mandore</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Mandavyapura</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mandore, anciently Mandavyapura, is a historic town a few kilometres north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jodhpur">Jodhpur</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. It was the seat of the Pratiharas of Mandavyapura from about the 6th century and was taken by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rathore">Rathores</a> around 1400 as their capital, until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rao-jodha">Rao Jodha</a> founded Jodhpur in 1459. Its gardens hold the cenotaphs of the Rathore rulers.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mandore.jpg" alt="Mandore" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jon Connell from Cambridge, UK · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Temples_at_Mandor_(4571805346).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Mandore lies 9 km north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jodhpur">Jodhpur</a> city in the Jodhpur district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a>, and is now a suburb as well as a historical town. It was the seat of the Pratiharas of Mandavyapura, who ruled the region in the 6th century CE, and a Pratihara family went on ruling there after the Gurjara-Pratihara empire broke up. Local belief holds it to be the native place of Mandodari, wife of Ravan, and a Ravan temple stands there.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>To defend its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiefdom">chiefdom</a> against the Tughluq dynasty of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a>, the Pratihara family allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rathore">Rathore</a> chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rao-chunda">Rao Chunda</a> (r. c. 1383–1424), who married a Pratihara princess and received the Mandore Fort as her dowry. Rao Ranmal Rathore took the throne of Mandore in 1427 and also served as administrator of Mewar for Maharana Mokal and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rana-kumbha">Rana Kumbha</a>. In 1438 Rana Kumbha had Ranmal assassinated in Chittor and seized Mandore.</p><p>Ranmal's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rao-jodha">Rao Jodha</a> escaped with about 700 horsemen, but after fighting near Chittor and at the Someshwar Pass only seven men were with him when he reached Mandore. He abandoned it and took refuge at Kahuni, near present-day Bikaner. For 15 years he tried to retake it, and in 1453, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rana-kumbha">Rana Kumbha</a> faced attacks by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultans</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, he took it by surprise. Mandore stayed the capital of Marwar until Rao Jodha moved it to his new city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jodhpur">Jodhpur</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Mandore remained the royal cremation ground, with the devals of every king of Marwar up to Maharaja Takht Singh. The ruined fort, built in stages, holds a large ruined temple with carvings of plants, birds, animals and planets. The Mandore gardens contain the chhatris of many rulers of Jodhpur State, among them that of Maharaja Ajit Singh, built in 1793, together with a government museum founded in 1968 in the Janana Mahal and a Hall of Heroes of 16 figures carved from a single rock.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mandore” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Several Pratihara and Gurjara lineages existed, including the Pratiharas of <b>Mandavyapura</b> near Jodhpur and the Gurjaras of Nandipuri in Gujarat, but the imperial line begins with Nagabhata I, whose base is variously placed at Ujjain or in the Jalor region.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gurjara-pratihara">Gurjara-Pratihara · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Rao Chunda took <b>Mandore</b> around 1395, and in 1459 Rao Jodha founded Jodhpur and the hilltop fort of Mehrangarh as a new capital.</blockquote><a href="/state/jodhpur-state">Jodhpur State</a></li><li><blockquote>They established themselves at <b>Mandavyapura</b>, modern Mandore near Jodhpur, in the 6th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/pratiharas-of-mandavyapura">Pratiharas of Mandavyapura</a></li></ul></section>
